From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sarol263@student.otago.ac.nz>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:48:03 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 31/49] Plutonium: broker follow-ups: grantrevoked,
 unsubscribing, NativeWindowEvent constructor

EXO-BROKER:
- The shell hears when a grant it holds stops working: a "grantrevoked"
  NativeWindowEvent (event.grant, event.windowId) after revokeGrant(), and
  when the window closes, just before its "windowremoved".
  WindowBrokerObserver.OnGrantRevoked, forwarded for the document's own
  grants only.
- Removing the last listener of a kind of broker event unsubscribes (the
  observer pipe closes and the browser drops it); listening again
  subscribes again, with a new snapshot. A snapshot that arrives after its
  subscription ended is dropped. For windows, tabs, groups, shells and
  context menus; a page that stops listening for context menus gets
  Chrome's own again.
- new NativeWindowEvent(type, {windowId, entry, grant}), and a grant
  attribute.
- ExoWindowBroker tells observers one event at a time. Revoking a grant
  detached the element, whose detach changed the window, which notified
  observers from inside the revocation's notification: a re-entrant
  ObserverList iteration, fatal with DCHECKs (revokeGrant() crashed the
  dev build). Events an observer causes now wait their turn, so the shell
  gets "grantrevoked" before the "windowchanged" it causes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
